A class is thread-safe if:
   - Multiple threads can use it safely
   - No race conditions
   - No data corruption
   - No inconsistent state

Java achieves this in 4 main ways:
   - Immutability
   - Synchronization
   - Atomic operations
   - Concurrent collections

Immutable = cannot change after creation.
 These are automatically thread-safe.
  Examples:
   - String
   - Integer
   - Long
   - Double
   - Boolean
   - BigDecimal
   - BigInteger
   - All wrapper classes
   - LocalDate, LocalDateTime (java.time package)

Synchronized Classes (Thread-Safe but Slower)
   - Vector
   - Hashtable
   - StringBuffer
   - Collections.synchronizedList(), synchronizedMap(), etc.

Atomic Classes (Thread-Safe with Better Performance)
    - AtomicInteger
    - AtomicLong
    - AtomicReference
    - AtomicBoolean
    - AtomicIntegerArray
    - AtomicLongArray
    - AtomicReferenceArray

Concurrent Collections (Thread-Safe with High Performance)
    - ConcurrentHashMap
    - CopyOnWriteArrayList
    - CopyOnWriteArraySet
    - ConcurrentLinkedQueue
    - ConcurrentLinkedDeque
    - LinkedBlockingQueue
    - ArrayBlockingQueue

Which are not Thread-Safe?
    - ArrayList
    - HashMap
    - HashSet
    - LinkedList
    - StringBuilder
    - Non-atomic wrapper classes (e.g., Integer, Long when modified)

Stack/Thread Stack: not shared between threads, each thread has its own stack
and is created when the thread starts
   - Local variables (primitives and references)
   - Method call frames (return address, parameters, local variables)
   - ThreadLocal variables (each thread has its own copy of a ThreadLocal variable, so they are thread-safe by design)

--------------

| Case                             | Thread-Safe? | Why                |
| -------------------------------- | ------------ | -----------------  |
| Local primitive                  | ✅ Yes        | Stack, not shared |
| Local object reference           | ✅ Yes        | Stack, not shared |
| Local mutable object             | ✅ Yes        | Not shared        |
| Static primitive                 | ❌ No         | Shared            |
| Static mutable object            | ❌ No         | Shared            |
| Shared immutable object (String) | ✅ Yes        | Cannot change     |